      "text": "To solve the logarithmic equation \\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\), let's first rewrite it in a simpler form by converting the bases to a common base. In this case, we can use the natural logarithm of both 7 and 8, but since we don't know if they are common bases, let's take the natural log of the equation.\n\nGiven:\n\\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\)\n\nTake the natural log of both sides:\n\\( \\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x) \\)\n\nUsing the property of logarithms, \\( \\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a) \\), we get:\n\\( (x+7) \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8) \\)\n\nDistribute the logarithms:\n\\( x \\ln(7) + 7 \\ln(7) = x \\ln(8) \\)\n\nRearrange to isolate \\( x \\):\n\\( x \\ln(7) - x \\ln(8) = -7 \\ln(7) \\)\n\nFactor out \\( x \\):\n\\( x (\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)) = -7 \\ln(7) \\)\n\nSolve for \\( x \\):\n\\( x = \\frac{-7 \\ln(7)}{\\ln(7) - \\ln(8)} \\)\n\nHowever, this approach seems complicated. Instead, let's try to solve it by equating exponentials directly. Write the equation:\n\\( 7^{x+7} = 8^x \\)\n\nThis implies:\n\\( (7 \\cdot 10^x) \\cdot 7^7 = 8^x \\)\n\nHence,\n\\( 7 \\cdot 10^x + 7^8 = 8^x \\ln(7) / \\ln(10^x) \\)\n\nLet's take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\\( \\ln(7 \\cdot 10^x + 7^8) = \\ln(8^x) \\)\n\nSince \\( 10^x \\) can be separated:\n\\( \\ln(7 \\cdot 10^x + 7^8) = \\ln(8^x) \\)\n\nLet's subtract \\(\\ln(8^x)\\) from both sides:\n\\( \\ln(7 \\cdot 10^x + 7^8) - \\ln(8^x) = 0 \\)\n\nSimplify by the properties of logarithms:\n\\( \\ln(7) + \\ln(10^x) - \\ln(8) = 0 \\)\n\nSo:\n\\( \\ln(7) + \\ln(10^x) - \\ln(8) = 0 \\)\n\nNow, let's isolate \\( \\ln(10^x) \\):\n\\( \\ln(10^x) = \\ln(8) - \\ln(7) \\)\n\nSince \\( 10^x = (2 \\cdot 5)^x = 2^x5^x \\), then:\n\\( \\ln(10^x) = \\ln(2^x) + x \\ln(5) \\)\n\nThen,\n\\( \\ln(8) - \\ln(7) = x \\ln(5) \\)\n\nHence,\n\\( x = \\frac{\\ln(8) - \\ln(7)}{\\ln(5)} \\)\n\nIn this form, look for the value:\n\\( x \\)\n\nHence, the final answer is:\n\\(\\boxed{5}\\)",
